A||||9||Philphine||welding for v8 or other swap||||1||
Z||000000||Philphine||08-27-2001||08:45 PM||||now that i have my hoist working and tried out (pulled the engine out of the chopper) i'm feeling a little more like an engine swap is possible. from what i've read some welding is required also (for a smallblock chevy swap anyway). i've done some at work and feel i could do the welding needed, but i can't take the parts to work. i'm wondering how big a welder i'd need to get the strenth required. would anything running off 120v get it done? if not what would i need. i'd be hoping to get a small wire fed welder 'cause thats what i have my bit of experience with. thanks for any help.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||08-27-2001||08:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B>i'm wondering how big a welder i'd need to get the strenth required. would anything running off 120v get it done? if not what would i need.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>IMO, you'd need at least 120 amps to weld the brackets for the engine mounts. I don't think you'll get good penetration with anything less. To get this much current, you'll have to get a 240 V welder.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||isthiswhereiputausername?||08-27-2001||09:05 PM||||I think anything about 90 would burn holes pretty easy. I find for frame welding that 75-90 for good steel is best..<P>Wire feed welder is great for welding thin steel and floors, but you should be able to use the 120volt one no problem.. may take you longer then the 240v one.. but it should be adaquate..<P>try welding a few chucks of scrap  steel for practice and see how it turns out..<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Archie||08-27-2001||09:07 PM||||If the only reason you are considering the purchase of a welder is to do a V-8 swap, then don't buy the welder.<P>For the 1st 8 years I was doing V-8 swaps, I didn't even have a welder. If done according to the video instruction tape, you can trial fit and then take the parts that are to be welded together over to a local Welding shop and pay the guy $25 to do the welding for you.  If you are on a budgit, that will save you quite a bit of $$$.<P>Archie ||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||08-28-2001||11:24 AM||||If you are buy a welder for just this swap then Archie has a good point. If, however, you are buying one for future use (or are just a tool junkie like me) I recommend spending a little more and buying a small MIG welder. It's much more flexible and so easy to use. I've got both. Since I bought the MIG the arc welder just collects dust.<P>My 2¢ worth,<BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy :D<BR>87 GT   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG"> ||||reg||6
Z||000005||Archie||08-28-2001||11:35 AM||||I agree with Roy.<P>Additionally, if you are looking for an excuse that you can use with the wife to justify the purchase of a good welder, then feel free to tell her that you have to do 20 hours of welding to get that engine in there. Heck, just blame it on me, I can handle it.<P>Archie  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||08-28-2001||11:49 AM||||shows how much i know. i thought a mig was a wire fed welder. what do you call a wire fed that uses the non flamable gas with it? tig? i think i would come up with other uses for it. i'm learning this stuff relatively late in life so it's kind of fun for me. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Joe Torma||08-28-2001||11:52 AM||||I have a $100 Cambell Housfeld wirefed welder.  I welded all sorts of brackets and mounts for my 3400 conversion.  Once I got the hang of it, I was welding 1/4"+ steel with near perfect beads and good penetration!  I never really welded before.   May not be the best welder, only 2 heat settings, but it worked great!<P><BR>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig.gif">  <p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 08-28-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||artherd||08-28-2001||05:31 PM||||A decent MIG welder will set you back about $500 (and thats for a no-name one, with gas)<P>You can probally weld with just a flux-wire as the cradle is steel, but..... well. MIG is better  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I'm actually going to pluck a used TIG off ebay methinks. Tools Rule.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000009||Jake_Dragon||08-28-2001||06:22 PM||||MIG = Metal Inert Gas welding (Wire Feed)<BR>TIG = Tungsten Inert Gas welding (Torch)<BR>SMAW = Shielded Metal Arc Welding (Stick)<BR>Oxy-acetylene welding can also be done but I would not trust it.<BR> <BR>You can also purchase flux core that will not require a shielding gas but will require a hotter welder. <BR>C02 and C02/Argon mix are common gasses used with mild steel.<BR>Argon for aluminum.<P>If it was my money I would get a Mig and use a flux-shielded gas combination.<P>You should check some rental places before you put any money down.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif"> Had to make room for a Smiley  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
